==============================================================================
2. IS d PREDICTABLE GIVEN offset?
==============================================================================

  taxi = offset - d, and offset is always observable. So for each
  offset band: how spread out is d, and how much of that spread is
  the model already capturing?

  offset band                n     sd(d)  sd(resid)  sd(taxi)   % MSE
  [0, 1,800)           203,854       443        243       356   30.7%
  [1,800, 3,600)        95,339       684        330       512   26.5%
  [3,600, 7,200)        33,357     1,193        467       740   18.5%
  [7,200, 14,400)        7,718     2,166        834     1,111   13.7%
  >= 14,400              1,383    16,208      1,683     7,292   10.1%

  sd(resid) << sd(d) means the model IS explaining d in that band.
  sd(resid) ~= sd(d) means it is predicting ~a constant and the
  spread is unexplained.

==============================================================================
3. IS THE LARGE-OFFSET POPULATION BIMODAL?
==============================================================================

  For large offsets a flight either pushed back near schedule and
  then sat (d ~ 0, taxi ~ offset) or pushed back very late (d ~
  offset, taxi ~ normal). If those are indistinguishable from
  observables, the conditional mean is already RMSE-optimal.

  offset >= 7200s: n=9,101 (23.8% of total MSE)

  taxi/offset ratio            n  % of band  median taxi   median d
  ~0  (pushed late)        4,170      45.8%          788     10,679
  0.1-0.5                  4,728      52.0%        1,270      7,197
  0.5-0.9                     80       0.9%        5,789      2,702
  ~1  (sat at stand)         116       1.3%        9,931          1

  A U-shape (mass at both ends) = mixture. The question is whether
  anything observable separates the two arms.

  existing echo_prob on each arm:
    pushed-late arm  (ratio<0.5):  mean echo_prob=0.005  n=8,898
    sat-at-stand arm (ratio>=0.9): mean echo_prob=0.559  n=123
    (well separated => the classifier already distinguishes them;
     similar => this is the unexploited signal)

